            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Judgment is primarily understood as a divine evaluative event—especially the final eschatological tribunal—where human deeds are assessed and a verdict of reward or condemnation is rendered, with vindication and temporal punishment as secondary themes.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Judgment is defined as the ruler's office, the law's ruling, and deliverance, operating as a broader kingly function of governance where condemnation is merely one possible verdict.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"An event of divine evaluation and verdict-rendering.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The office of the ruler and the execution of the law to govern, deliver, and establish justice.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book expands the concept of judgment from a primarily courtroom or punitive evaluation to encompass the full scope of a ruler's governance, including saving the oppressed and fighting battles.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly rejects equating judgment solely with condemnation, asserting that condemnation is 'one verdict of the office, not the office.'\",\n  \"rationale\": \"While the consensus acknowledges vindication as a secondary aspect of judgment, it primarily defines the term as an evaluative event. The book broadens the core definition to mean the kingly office of ruling and delivering, making the consensus's primary focus (condemnation/verdict) a mere subset of its broader definition.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The New Testament frequently uses 'judgment' (krisis) to refer specifically to the final eschatological tribunal and its resulting punitive or rewarding verdict, which functions distinctly from general kingly governance.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
